  Fourth question, and it's the last one that changes the design — what happens when the page loads on a hostname the
  map doesn't recognize (a preview deploy, a new vanity domain, someone's LAN IP hitting the dev server).

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether an unrecognized host silently gets a working default, or refuses to guess.

  - [1mFall back to production, warn loudly[0m — unknown host resolves to the production endpoint and logs a [38;5;153mconsole.warn[39m
    naming the hostname. Buys: the page always works; the warning makes the gap findable during the one session where it
    matters. Costs: a mistyped or forgotten host quietly talks to prod, and a warning nobody reads is nearly the same
    as silence.
  - [1mFall back to production, silently[0m — same behavior, no warning. Buys: nothing you can't get from the option above.
    Costs: strictly worse — same prod risk, no signal.
  - [1mThrow on unknown host[0m — the module raises at load; the page fails fast with a clear message. Buys: an unlisted
    environment can never point at prod by accident, and the failure names exactly what to fix. Costs: a forgotten map
    entry breaks a preview deploy outright instead of degrading.

  [1mMy recommendation: fall back to production with a warning.[0m In a real app I'd argue for throwing — accidental prod
  traffic is the expensive failure. But this login is a stub that never issues a request, so the prod risk is currently
  theoretical while a hard throw would break the page for real. The warning keeps the gap visible, and tightening it to
  a throw later is a two-line change with no interface impact.
